# What upstream's omarchy-base.packages names that this image does not
# install, and why. One line per package, every one accounted for.
#
# This file is documentation, not input -- nothing reads it. It exists because
# a package set with silent holes is a package set nobody can audit, and
# because the interesting question about an aarch64 port is exactly which names
# did not survive: 27 are not in Arch Linux ARM, and 25 of those are left out.
#
# Checked against Arch Linux ARM's aarch64 core/extra with `pacman -Si`.

# --- Omarchy's own repo, which is x86_64-only -----------------------------
#   https://pkgs.omarchy.org/stable/x86_64/omarchy.db -> 200 (211 packages)
#   https://pkgs.omarchy.org/stable/aarch64/omarchy.db -> 404
# These are Basecamp's own packages. Each would have to be built from source
# for aarch64; none of them decides whether the session starts, so phase 1
# leaves them out and phase 2 can pick off whichever turn out to matter.
aether          # Omarchy's mail client
cliamp          # TUI music player
herdr           # remote/agent bridge; NOT the shell (that is quickshell) and
                # not closed source -- moarchy's docs/omarchy-4x-feasibility.md
                # is the correction to the opposite claim
omacalc         # calculator
omacut          # snippet/shortcut tool
omawrite        # writing app
omarchy-nvim    # Omarchy's Neovim distribution
tensaku         # text tooling
tobi-try        # scratch runner
ttfx            # font tool

# --- in the AUR, not in Arch Linux ARM ------------------------------------
# All three have PKGBUILDs that build cleanly for aarch64 -- moarchy already
# builds every one of them in its own container. They are omitted rather than
# blocked: vm/build-packages.sh builds any [pkg.*] the manifest pins, so adding
# one is a pin and a line in vm/packages/apps.
#
# xdg-terminal-exec was the fourth, and left this list on 2026-09-11: every
# terminal Omarchy opens goes through it, and Settings bridges forty-odd rows
# that way. It is [pkg.xdg-terminal-exec] in the manifest and installed with the
# apps tier.
#
# mise-bin was the fifth, and left it on 2026-09-12. Every route to a coding
# agent runs through mise -- omarchy-default-agent installs with it, and
# omarchy-mise-install's wrappers resolve through it -- so while it was omitted
# the AI agent row was guarded off and the drawer had nothing to offer for
# "agent" at all. It is [pkg.mise-bin] in the manifest and installed with the
# apps tier.
ttf-ia-writer         # the theme fonts want it; noto covers the gap
yay                   # AUR helper -- nothing to help with inside this image yet
ufw-docker            # needs docker+ufw integration we are not exercising
tzupdate              # geo timezone; the VM's clock comes from the host

# --- exists under a different name in Arch ---------------------------------
nvim                  # Omarchy's repo name for its Neovim; Arch has `neovim`,
                      # which the apps tier does NOT install because Omarchy's
                      # config for it lives in omarchy-nvim above. Left out
                      # rather than half-installed.

# --- x86_64-only upstreams -------------------------------------------------
obsidian              # proprietary, no aarch64 build
dotnet-runtime        # no aarch64 package in Arch Linux ARM
localsend             # ships x86_64 binaries
obs-studio            # in [extra] for x86_64 only
pinta                 # depends on dotnet-runtime, above

# --- hardware this VM does not have ----------------------------------------
asdcontrol                      # Apple Studio Display brightness over USB
hyprland-preview-share-picker   # screencast source picker; no aarch64 build,
                                # and xdg-desktop-portal-hyprland still works
qemu-user-static-binfmt         # cross-arch binfmt -- we ARE the other arch
yaru-icon-theme                 # not in ALARM; gnome-themes-extra covers the
                                # GTK side and Omarchy themes the rest

# --- available for aarch64, left out on purpose ----------------------------
plymouth                        # in ALARM, but deliberately not installed: it
                                # hides the console on a VM whose console is
                                # the main way in when a boot goes wrong
sddm                            # in ALARM, and installed by the session tier's
                                # dependencies is NOT what happens -- it is left
                                # out entirely. A phone does not show a login
                                # screen, and tty1 autologin into `uwsm start
                                # hyprland` is both what moarchy ships and one
                                # fewer graphical thing that can fail before
                                # Hyprland gets a chance to.
#
# Upstream's desktop apps that are not in a phone's default set, and the
# previewer that would pull one of them back in. All seven install fine from
# ALARM. They left the apps tier on 2026-09-11 together with evince, and those
# six took 141 packages and 400 MB off its download; Print Settings and Disks,
# dropped later that day, another 5 packages and 2.6 MB. GNOME's phone apps
# took their place (vm/packages/apps). evince is the one that came back: on
# 2026-09-12 chromium left, and with it the mimeinfo-cache entry that had been
# answering application/pdf by accident. `pacman -S` brings any of the rest
# back.
libreoffice-fresh               # desktop office suite; 163 MB on its own
kdenlive                        # desktop video editor; 183 MB with the KDE
                                # Frameworks it pulls in
moonlight-qt                    # game-streaming client
xournalpp                       # stylus notebook; Notes (moarchy-keep) is the
                                # note-taking app here
sushi                           # Nautilus's spacebar previewer; a phone has no
                                # spacebar. (Until 2026-09-12 this line also
                                # said installing it drags evince back in --
                                # evince is in the apps tier now, so that
                                # objection is gone and only the spacebar one
                                # is left.)
system-config-printer           # Print Settings, a desktop's printer admin.
                                # CUPS and its web interface stay
gnome-disk-utility              # Disks; partitioning is not a phone's job

# --- replaced, not merely dropped ------------------------------------------
# The one package here that upstream's set names, that installs and runs fine
# for aarch64, and that this image swaps for a different one rather than doing
# without. Measured on the built image at the 360x674 panel, 2026-09-12:
#
#   - Chromium has no narrow mode. Its own first-run Terms dialog is a
#     fixed-width desktop window: at 360px the text is clipped off the right
#     edge and no accept button is reachable. It was also the only window
#     chromium ever mapped -- with --no-first-run, and again with
#     --disable-gpu, the browser window never appeared, and the log says
#     `ContextResult::kTransientFailure: Failed to send
#     GpuControl.CreateCommandBuffer`. That is the VM's missing hardware EGL,
#     the same thing that makes mpv abort, so on real phone hardware chromium
#     would probably start -- and would still have no narrow layout, which is
#     the half of this that no GPU fixes.
#   - Epiphany switches itself to a phone layout at that width: URL bar at the
#     bottom, back/forward/tabs/library as a bottom toolbar. It loaded a real
#     page at 360px with no argument or configuration.
#   - Epiphany costs 3.59 MiB of download and 17.59 MiB installed, and adds no
#     dependency the image did not already have. Chromium cost 117.28 and
#     434.36 -- by itself nearly a quarter of the apps tier's download.
#
# Angelfish, the other genuinely mobile-first browser in ALARM's aarch64
# [extra], was measured too and not taken: 15 of its 17 dependencies are
# missing here, including qt6-webengine at 81.27 MiB, for ~91 MiB of download
# and ~341 MiB installed. That is the KDE Frameworks cascade this file already
# refused once, four lines up, when it dropped kdenlive. Firefox (66.87 MiB)
# has no adaptive UI on desktop Linux at all, and qutebrowser is keyboard-driven.
#
# What this costs, and what pays it back:
#   - upstream's mimeapps.list names chromium.desktop for http/https, so the
#     image ships default/etc/skel/.config/mimeapps.list naming Epiphany.
#   - omarchy-launch-webapp hardcodes a chromium.desktop fallback for every
#     browser outside the chrome family, so every web app tile would exec
#     nothing. default/usr/local/bin/omarchy-launch-webapp shadows it.
#   - PDFs lose their accidental handler, which is why evince is in the apps
#     tier now.
#   - upstream's hypr/apps/browser.lua and pip.lua tag chromium-family windows
#     for opacity and Meet's PiP. Those rules simply never match now. They are
#     upstream config this project vendors unpatched, and cosmetic, so they
#     stay as they are.
#   - install/user/chromium.sh installs the copy-url and yt-dlp native
#     messaging hosts. This image never runs upstream's installer, so those
#     were never set up here and nothing is lost.
#
# `pacman -S chromium` brings it back, and the Settings Browser page has a
# Chromium row waiting behind a guard for exactly that.
chromium                        # upstream's browser; replaced by epiphany
